cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use swiftld, please cite it as below, and also cite vcftools, whose behaviour it reproduces."
title: "swiftld: a byte-identical drop-in replacement for vcftools pairwise linkage disequilibrium"
type: software
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-22
license: LGPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/joe-b-christensen/swiftld"
authors:
  - family-names: Christensen
    given-names: Joe
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-0908-1299"
    email: joechristensen@clin.au.dk
    affiliation: "Translational Neuropsychiatry Unit (TNU), Department of Clinical Medicine, Aarhus University, Denmark"
keywords:
  - linkage disequilibrium
  - vcftools
  - population genetics
  - performance
abstract: >-
  swiftld computes pairwise linkage disequilibrium (vcftools --hap-r2 and --geno-r2) with output byte-identical to
  vcftools 0.1.17 for every supported option and thread count. On three 1000 Genomes regions it was 826x faster
  than vcftools and 7.6x faster than PLINK 2, which writes a different file (geometric means, two threads,
  AVX-512 CPU).
references:
  - type: article
    title: "The variant call format and VCFtools"
    authors:
      - family-names: Danecek
        given-names: Petr
      - name: "et al."
    journal: Bioinformatics
    year: 2011
    volume: 27
    start: 2156
    end: 2158
    doi: "10.1093/bioinformatics/btr330"
